<article class="env-lemma" id="0474">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0474">Lemma <span data-tag="0474">29.33.16</span>.</a> Let $i : Z \to X$ be an immersion of schemes over $S$, and assume $i$ (locally) has a left inverse. Then the canonical sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \mathcal{C}_{Z/X} \to i^*\Omega _{X/S} \to \Omega _{Z/S} \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> of Lemma <a href="/tag/01UZ" data-tag="01UZ">29.33.15</a> is (locally) split exact. In particular, if $s : S \to X$ is a section of the structure morphism $X \to S$ then the map $\mathcal{C}_{S/X} \to s^*\Omega _{X/S}$ induced by $\text{d}_{X/S}$ is an isomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/02HP" data-tag="02HP">10.131.10</a> that the result holds when $X, Z, S$ are affine. In general we conclude the sequence is locally split exact. If $g : X \to Z$ is a left inverse of $i$, then $i^*c_ g$ is a right inverse of the map $i^*\Omega _{X/S} \to \Omega _{Z/S}$ by Modules, Lemma <a href="/tag/01UW" data-tag="01UW">17.28.13</a>. This implies the sequence is split by Homology, Lemma <a href="/tag/010G" data-tag="010G">12.5.10</a>. Finally, if $s$ is a section, then it is an immersion $s : Z = S \to X$ over $S$ (see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KT" data-tag="01KT">26.21.11</a>) and in that case $\Omega _{Z/S} = 0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>